<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Valentin Vodnik</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1758">1758</a>–<a href="/years/1819">1819</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Valentin Vodnik was a Slovene priest, poet and journalist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carniola">Carniola</a>. He edited the first Slovene-language newspaper, Lublanske novice, from 1797 to 1800, and wrote the first Slovene poems of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. Under the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyrian-provinces">Illyrian Provinces</a> he directed schools teaching in Slovene and praised the new order in his ode <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a> Revived, which cost him his post after Austrian rule returned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/valentin-vodnik.jpg" alt="Valentin Vodnik" width="320" height="363" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz von Kurz zum Thurn und Goldenstein · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Kurz_zum_Thurn_und_Goldenstein_-_Valentin_Vodnik_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
